The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
final HashSet<Integer> testSet = Sets.newHashSet();
testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
```
Reason:
The `testSet` is being accessed by multiple threads without synchronization, which can lead to race conditions when multiple threads attempt to modify it simultaneously. This can cause inconsistencies in the elements of the `testSet`, as concurrent modifications can result in lost updates or corrupted data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
synchronized (testSet) {
    testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
}
```
Reason:
By wrapping the modification of `testSet` in a synchronized block, we ensure that only one thread can modify the set at a time. This prevents race conditions and guarantees that the state of `testSet` remains consistent across concurrent accesses.

